  "Number": 8,
  "Question": "If the radius of a circle is increased \\(100\\%,\\) the area is increased:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(100\\%\\)",
    "B": "\\(200\\%\\)",
    "C": "\\(300\\%\\)",
    "D": "\\(400\\%\\)",
    "E": "By none of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "An increase of \\(100\\%\\) adds the entire original radius, so the radius doubles. Let the original radius be \\(r>0\\) and the original area be \\(A=\\pi r^2>0\\). The new area is\n\\[\n\\pi(2r)^2=4\\pi r^2=4A.\n\\]\nThe increase is \\(4A-A=3A\\). To express that increase as a percentage, divide by the original area:\n\\[\n\\frac{3A}{A}\\cdot100\\%=300\\%.\n\\]\nThus the area increases by \\(\\boxed{300\\%}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
